# ===== Environment variables / Secrets =====
# Deliberately NOT ignored: .env is committed so the mentor can clone and run the
# project with no extra setup. The patterns below stay commented out on purpose --
# uncomment them if this repo is ever reused with real credentials.
#.env
#.env.local
#*.env
#config/secrets.*

# ===== Data files =====
# Rule for every data/output directory below: ignore the CONTENTS, keep the
# DIRECTORY. Each one is created by a tracked .gitkeep, because the pipeline
# writes into these paths and several modules would fail on a fresh clone if the
# directory were absent (the FileHandler in each module, and save_rejected).
#
# The raw CSV is supplied separately by the mentor, so it is never committed.
data/raw/*
!data/raw/.gitkeep

# data/processed/ holds two kinds of generated artifact, both ignored:
#   - the six normalized tables (dim_*.csv, fact_*.csv) written by
#     load.save_processed_tables()
#   - the rejected-record CSVs written by load.save_rejected()
# They are NOT missing from the repo -- run the pipeline and they appear.
# Measured on a 200k-row slice and scaled: fact_sales.csv lands around 81 MB and
# fact_inventory_snapshot.csv around 28 MB for the full 1M-row input (~113 MB for
# all six). That is past GitHub's 50 MB warning and close enough to its 100 MB
# hard limit that committing them is a bad idea regardless.
#
# Note the pattern is `data/processed/*` and not `data/processed/`: a bare
# directory pattern stops git from descending into it at all, which makes any
# later `!` negation unreachable.
data/processed/*
!data/processed/.gitkeep
!data/processed/rejected_records/
data/processed/rejected_records/*
!data/processed/rejected_records/.gitkeep

# ===== Generated outputs =====
# The five Matplotlib charts are regenerated by every run of reports.py, so only
# the directory is tracked.
reports/charts/*
!reports/charts/.gitkeep

# ===== Docker =====
# (docker-compose.yml and Dockerfile SHOULD be committed - only ignore volumes/data)
postgres_data/

# ===== IDE / Editor / tooling =====
.vscode/
.idea/
.claude/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store

# ===== Jupyter =====
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# ===== Logs =====
# `logs/*` rather than `logs/`, same reason as data/processed above -- the .gitkeep has to
# stay tracked or the FileHandler in every module raises on import in a fresh clone.
logs/*
!logs/.gitkeep